  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe strategy employs a convex relaxation approach using semidefinite programming (SDP) to tighten the upper bound on C\u2085. By approximating the problem with an SDP, we can leverage convex optimization to find a lower bound on the maximum overlap, indirectly improving the upper bound on C\u2085. This approach addresses the non-convexity of the original problem and avoids local optima issues faced by gradient-based methods.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe approximate the problem using a sum-of-squares (SOS) relaxation, formulating it as an SDP:\n- Variables: h \u2208 \u211d\u207f (step function values)\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085\n- Constraints:\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  - \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2\n  - For all k, \u222bh(x)(1 - h(x + k))dx \u2264 C\u2085\nThis is relaxed to a convex form by bounding h in a reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS) and using a grid of shifts for tractability.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialize: Use initial_h_values or a uniform h. Discretize h into n_points (e.g., 70).\nb. Formulate SDP: Use CVXOPT solver to minimize C\u2085 with shifted correlation constraints.\nc. Refine: Apply adaptive grid refinement around high-correlation shifts and adjust bounds.\nd. Evaluate: Ensure feasibility and update the best solution.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Grid resolution: Start with n_points=70, increase if time permits.\n- Shifts per iteration: Begin with 100, adaptively focus on high-correlation regions.\n- SOS relaxation order: Use degree 2 to balance accuracy and speed.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nThe SDP solves in ~1000s for n_points=70. Adaptive shifts reduce the problem size without sacrificing accuracy.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEnsure h meets constraints and the returned C\u2085 bound is valid.",
